            bool assertion = (New_Ret == false && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListIndexOfX) || New_Ret == true;
            AssumeProperty.True(
            assertion,
            new object[]
            {
                New_Ret,
                Old_arrListLastIndexOfX,
                New_arrListIndexOfX
            });
        }
    }
}
```
This test method asserts that if `Contains` returns false, the last index of `x` in the old list is less than or equal to the index of `x` in the new list. If `Contains` returns true, there's no additional condition to hold.

The `AssumeProperty.True()` function states that the property associated with the provided parameter must be true. The parameter is a condition and if it's false, the method throws `InvalidOperationException`. The condition is created based on `assertion` variable.

The `PexObserve.ValueForViewing()` method logs the values to be viewed during debugging.

Please note, you should run ArrayListContractTest with Microsoft Visual Studio 2012 and NUnit Test Adapter and Pex 4.3.73. For more information, you may check the official website of Microsoft Practical Extensible Testing (Pex) tool.

Testing this method should generate a large number of test cases and verify the correctness of the public `bool Contains(T item)` method. The contract of the method states that it will always succeed, so in a real-world situation it would always return true or false, indicating whether the element is in the `ArrayList` or not.

To make testing easier and more efficient, the Pex tool provides built-in methods and properties to test various aspects of types and methods. Sometimes, it’s necessary to make assumptions about the preconditions and postconditions of a method. The `Assume` utility class is used for these purposes. By setting assumptions, Pex creates test cases with preconditions that don't meet those assumptions, which helps prevent test case failures.

By using these features, the Pex tool generates a large number of valid and invalid test scenarios, ensuring maximum coverage of possible behaviors and exceptions.

In conclusion, Pex automates the manual process of generating test cases and provides numerous built-in methods to express conditions and assertions. It is particularly useful when we want to test the behavior of objects and methods, especially when the code logic is complex and not always predictable. This tool offers an efficient and effective way to verify if a specific method or property works as expected in various test scenarios.

