### 4. Example of How to Use PexSymbolicValue Attribute and PexAssume Valid values Attribute 

This is usually used for testing methods which deal with boundaries or valid values.

Suppose there is a method named "CalculateTotalPayment" which takes a "rate" and "months".

The method should return a result only if the rate is between 0 and 1 and the number of months is more than 0.

```csharp
[PexMethod]
public decimal CalculateTotalPayment(decimal rate, int months)
{
    [PexAssumeValidValues(1,10)]
    [PexSymbolicValue] 
    decimal symbolRate = rate;

    [PexAssumeValidValues(1,10)]
    [PexSymbolicValue]decimal symbolMonths = months;
    
    if (rate >= 0 && rate <= 1 && months > 0)
    {
        // The calculation logic is not important here. Just return a pseudo value.
        return symbolRate * symbolMonths;
    }
    else throw new ArgumentException("Invalid parameters");
}
```
In this scenario, PexSymbolicValue is used to create symbolic variables for 'rate' and 'months'. PexAssumeValidValues is used to assume valid values for the parameters. 

But in this case, there is a small problem, since PexAssumeValidValues doesn't actually impact the method - it assumes a range, but doesn't ensure that parameters will fall into this range - it is more of a tool for Pex data generation. If method is called with parameters outside of the provided range, it will be executed with those parameters and assertion will fail (because we are assuming that parameters are always valid). 

### 5. PexInlineAttribute 

This attribute is used to replace a method call with a constant value or a reference to a static field or property.

Example:

```csharp
public class Mathematics
{
    public int Add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
}

[PexClass]
public class MathematicsTest
{
    [PexMethod]
    public void TestAddMethod(int x, int y)
    {
        Mathematics m = new Mathematics();
        int sum = m.Add(x, y);  // Method call would be replaced with: 3

        Assert.AreEqual(3, sum);
    }
}
```
Here, PexInlineAttribute is not needed, because PexTest Framework can automatically substitute the actual method call with the result of the method (in this case, 3). However, in complex situations, it can be handy to control the result of a method using the PexInlineAttribute. It is used when we know the outcome of a method without the complexity of setting up a test data, as seen with the example above, but it is most useful when testing methods which can't usually be set up as a test data (like Random() method which can't usually be setup to return a fixed value).

### 6. PexObserve Attribute

This attribute is used when you want to record the information for later use or viewing. It will create a snapshot of the values you pass to the method, so that in the next call of--------------------            // @@@ If the return value is true, last index of x in old arrList is < index of x in new arrList. 
	    // @@@ The Count in old arrList is less than or equal to the Count in new arrList.
	    // This is to ensure that when the 'Contains' method returns false, it doesn't leave the list in a state where it is possible to have 'true' returned for a subsequent call. @@@ 
	    // @@@ This is a simplification for the 'Contains' contract. 
	    // @@@ For a full 'Contains' contract, 'Contains[obj] is always true or always false' does not appear to hold.
	    // The method 'Contains' is not specified in the PexContractAttribute.ContractTestCases  
	    // @@@ To complete the PexContract tests, add the contract @'[ContractTestCase(typeof(Predicate<T>), "(Predicate<T> p) returns true iff p(value) is true for every element in the collection", typeof(Predicate<T>))]' to the PexContractAttribute.ContractTestCases. 
        }
    }
}

